<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=radioactivity">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radioactivity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Radioactive decay, Nuclear decay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Radioactivity, or radioactive decay, is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by radiation, discovered in 1896 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel">Henri Becquerel</a> when uranium salts blackened a photographic plate wrapped in black paper. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-curie">Marie Curie</a> showed that the rays were a property of atoms; with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a> she coined the term &quot;radioactivity&quot; and isolated polonium and radium. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford">Ernest Rutherford</a> and Frederick Soddy were the first to realise that many decay processes turn one element into another. Decay is used to estimate the ages of rocks and organic matter.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radioactivity.jpg" alt="Radioactivity" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Becquerel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Becquerel_plate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Radioactive decay, also called nuclear decay or radioactivity, is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by radiation. The three most common types are alpha, beta and gamma decay. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford">Ernest Rutherford</a> named them in increasing order of their ability to penetrate matter, after early researchers found that an electric or magnetic field could split radioactive emissions into three beams. Beta decay is seen in all elements, while alpha decay is observed only in heavier elements of atomic number 52 and above.</p><p>For single atoms the process is random: according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">quantum theory</a> it is impossible to predict when a particular atom will decay. For many identical atoms the overall rate is expressed as a decay constant or a half-life, and half-lives range from nearly instantaneous to far longer than the age of the universe. Except in gamma decay, the process transmutes the nucleus, and when the number of protons changes an atom of a different element is created. Lead, with atomic number 82, is the heaviest element with stable isotopes. Twenty-eight naturally occurring elements are radioactive, among them uranium and thorium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Radioactivity was discovered in 1896 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel">Henri Becquerel</a>, who was working with phosphorescent materials. He wrapped a photographic plate in black paper and put various phosphorescent salts on it. Results were negative until he used uranium salts, which blackened the plate despite the paper. In 1897 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-curie">Marie Curie</a> began studying the radiation from uranium compounds, named it rayons de Becquerel, and showed that the rays were a property of atoms. The SI unit of activity, the becquerel, equals one decay per second.</p><p>The dangers were slow to be recognised. Physicians and companies marketed radioactive substances as patent medicines, including radium enemas and radium waters, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-curie">Marie Curie</a> warned that radium is dangerous in untrained hands. She later died of aplastic anaemia, likely caused by radiation. By the 1930s, after cases of bone necrosis and death among enthusiasts, such products had largely left the market. The first International Congress of Radiology met in 1925 to consider protection standards, and in 1927 Hermann Joseph Muller published research showing genetic effects, for which he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>An atomic battery uses the energy of decay to generate electric power without a chain reaction. Such batteries are costly but long-lived, and power equipment that must run unattended for long periods, such as spacecraft and pacemakers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Radioactive decay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He had discovered <b>radioactivity</b>, soon investigated by Marie and Pierre Curie.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As Marie Curie she pioneered research on <b>radioactivity</b> and became the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two sciences.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
